        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Are refurbished treadmills worth it?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Often, when they come from the manufacturer or an established dealer with a written, in-home warranty and a list of the parts replaced. They typically cost meaningfully less than new and include delivery and cover a private sale does not. Without a warranty and a work list, a refurbished treadmill is simply a used one and should be priced and inspected that way.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the difference between a refurbished and a used treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A properly refurbished treadmill has been inspected and serviced by a business, with worn parts such as the belt or deck replaced, and is sold with a warranty. A used treadmill is sold as seen, usually privately, with no warranty and no recourse. Some private listings call a cleaned machine refurbished, so judge by the warranty and the work list rather than the label.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much cheaper are refurbished treadmills?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              As typical ranges, open-box machines are about 10 to 25 percent below the current new price, manufacturer-refurbished home machines roughly 20 to 40 percent below, and dealer-refurbished ex-commercial machines can be 40 to 70 percent below their original cost. Compare against a new machine on sale, not the list price, before deciding a refurbished one is a bargain.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Do refurbished treadmills come with a warranty?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Those from manufacturers and reputable dealers do, but terms range from the full original period down to 90 days, often cover parts only, and may exclude wear items. Check the length, whether labour is included, whether service is in-home or return-to-base, and get it in writing against the serial number of your machine.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a refurbished commercial treadmill good for home use?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be excellent value, because commercial machines are built for heavier use and designed to be repaired. Check that your home can supply the power it needs, that it fits through your doorways and into the room since most do not fold, and that parts are still supported for the model.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What should be replaced on a refurbished treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              At minimum the belt should be lubricated and tensioned, worn belts and decks replaced or decks flipped, the motor compartment cleaned, and the motor, board, console and safety key tested. Drive belts and rollers should be inspected and replaced if worn. Ask the seller for the list of work done.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

